WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:01.601
I was

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.136
very clear that

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.505
the person running against Hillary

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.474
Clinton was not up to the job,

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.542
that it was going to be chaotic,

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.178
that the job of president is not a joke

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.281
and that it would cause problems.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.550
And to see citizens

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:19.853
storming through the Capitol

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:22.422
It was surreal and it was frightening

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.825
and deeply, deeply disappointing.

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.294
Former first lady Michelle

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.362
Obama is sharing her

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.730
thoughts on January six

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:32.532
last night with Stephen Colbert.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:33.533
Her new book, The Light

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.168
We Carry, is out today.

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:35.535
In it,

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:36.836
she speaks very bluntly

00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:38.071
about how she felt when former

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:40.106
President Trump won the 2016

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.575
presidential election.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.413
It shook me profoundly

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:46.846
to hear

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:47.547
the man

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:49.049
who replaced my husband

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:50.383
as president

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.019
openly and unapologetically

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:56.956
using ethnic slurs, making selfishness

00:00:56.956 --> 00:00:59.659
and hate somehow acceptable,

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:03.096
refusing to condemn white supremacists

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:04.731
or to support people

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:07.167
demonstrating for racial justice.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:09.869
It shocked me to hear him

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:11.137
speaking about

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:14.040
differentness as if it were a threat.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.876
It felt like something more,

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:17.977
something much

00:01:17.977 --> 00:01:20.847
uglier than a simple political defeat

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:24.384
for perspective on that comment and more.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:25.018
Let's bring in

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:26.619
NPR's All Things Considered,

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:27.387
Juana Summers.

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:29.989
She sat down with Michelle Obama.

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:30.423
And thank you

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.125
so much for joining us this morning

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:34.027
on what is a fascinating conversation,

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.296
just to hear her speak out so candidly.

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:38.164
And one thing I noticed

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.766
from your conversation with her is

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.667
the tone of this

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:41.668
book is a lot different

00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.103
than the tone of the other book

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.438
that people might be familiar with.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:46.473
That's absolutely right.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.508
It is still partially a memoir.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:50.143
She peels back the layers,

00:01:50.143 --> 00:01:51.511
much like she does in her acclaimed

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:52.112
book, Becoming

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.114
which came out back in 2018.

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.748
But she told me that she intentionally

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:56.816
wanted this book

00:01:56.816 --> 00:01:57.817
to be more practical,

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.519
to offer people a guidebook,

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.021
a road map of sorts, to chart a path out

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.556
during difficult times.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.191
She told me that she wrote this book

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:07.393
largely in isolation during the pandemic,

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:09.162
with only a close group of people

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:10.964
by her side who were giving input.

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:11.464
And it gave her

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:12.799
a lot of time to look inward

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:13.666
and think about

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:14.734
what type of tools

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:15.668
she could offer

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:16.803
to the type of people

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:17.504
across the country

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:18.104
and around the world

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:19.839
who constantly are writing her in

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:21.474
asking her for advice on how to solve

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:22.742
some of the biggest problems

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.978
that they're experiencing in their lives.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.113
When I think of Michelle Obama,

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.548
I think of bravery,

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:29.883
and that's just like what comes to mind.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.284
But what I love is in this book,

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.185
she opens up about

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:34.654
when she was really scared and fearful

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.623
and when she almost let that fear stand

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:37.724
in the way of history.

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:38.458
Let's listen to that

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:39.692
and get your thoughts on the other side.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:40.126
Here it is.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:43.463
I think the

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:46.132
most anxious I've ever been in my life

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:47.934
was when Barack told me

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.369
for the first time

00:02:49.369 --> 00:02:50.069
that he wanted

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:50.503
to run

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.373
for president of the United States.

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.308
I found the prospect of it

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:59.012
actually terrifying and perhaps worse

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:01.714
as we carried on a conversation off

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:02.115
and on

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:03.950
over the course of a few weeks

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.519
late in 2006.

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:07.620
He made it clear

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.523
that the decision was really up to me.

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.392
It's strange

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:13.126
to think

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:14.894
that I could have altered

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:18.198
the course of history with my fear,

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:22.068
but I didn't I said yes.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.537
100% true.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:26.506
I love it. 100% true.

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:27.540
What do you think?

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:29.375
I think it's something, etc.

00:03:29.375 --> 00:03:30.276
Even though you're talking about

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:31.778
someone who's a former first lady, it's

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:33.446
just this really relatable sentiment

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:34.380
for anyone

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.882
who's been in a long term relationship.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.116
Anyone who's in a marriage,

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:37.750
where in marriage

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:38.851
you have to have those give

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:40.253
and take those conversations.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:41.254
To hear her

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:42.222
openly acknowledge

00:03:42.222 --> 00:03:43.189
that she was fearful

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.924
of what this would mean for her family,

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.759
her children, her career,

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:47.994
how this would catapult

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.196
their life is a really unique revelation.

00:03:50.196 --> 00:03:50.730
Then, of course,

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.165
seeing the ways

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:53.733
in which their family was examined

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.802
and cross-examined and scrutinized

00:03:55.802 --> 00:03:56.669
during their eight years

00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:57.403
in the White House.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:57.870
In a sense,

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.973
I thought it was a really telling moment

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:00.573
to hear her

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:01.174
open up

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:03.676
and own that moment of intimidation.

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:04.410
Yeah.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:05.311
So you want to here's

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:06.546
the interesting thing.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:07.413
You know, when I was telling Poppy,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.181
that's 100%

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:08.681
truth is

00:04:08.681 --> 00:04:09.816
I was

00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:11.317
the local anchor in Chicago

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:13.353
when Barack Obama was a state senator,

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:14.554
and then he became senator

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.623
and then he ran for president.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.925
And I remember that this book,

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:20.526
long story short,

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:22.362
is the Michelle Obama that I know.

00:04:22.362 --> 00:04:23.663
Not that I'm like best friends with them,

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:24.230
but I do know

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.431
that likes to introduce them

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:26.299
like chicken dinners

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:27.500
as a local anchor, like,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.801
you know, Barack and Michelle Obama.

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:31.137
And so this was the

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:32.238
Michelle Obama

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.673
that Chicagoans knew,

00:04:33.673 --> 00:04:35.341
that the constituents knew.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.010
She didn't want to run for president.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.478
No, not And even

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:41.180
she had a hard time actually running

00:04:42.081 --> 00:04:44.284
and being for becoming being first lady.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.753
And so this is a book becoming

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:47.920
this great is a great book.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.088
There's no shade to Michelle Obama.

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.657
This is all praise for her.

00:04:50.657 --> 00:04:52.792
But this is the real book.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:55.561
This is what she really thinks.

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:57.363
This is personal

00:04:57.363 --> 00:04:58.464
and

00:04:58.698 --> 00:04:59.232
I think

00:04:59.232 --> 00:05:01.834
that this is actually more relatable

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:05.238
to women, you know, who have careers

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:06.806
and then have to sort of sometimes

00:05:06.806 --> 00:05:08.241
take a backseat to their husbands

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:08.941
because their house,

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:10.710
they put their husbands careers first.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.445
And exactly how she felt

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:13.713
about Donald Trump

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.181
and what he did to the country.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:15.648
This is it.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:17.250
You saw her face on inauguration day.

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.552
And this book is a reflection of that.

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:20.720
I think that's absolutely right.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.422
And you heard her to describe her family

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:24.957
in this book as this hopeful and earnest

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.993
black family that believed in hard work

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.861
that catapulted into the White House.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.630
And then after eight years

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:31.597
of what she describes

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.433
as progress and achieving

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:35.335
and believing in those principles

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:36.769
for the country to in her

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:38.438
words, has repudiated

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:39.605
what her family stand for.

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:40.807
She expresses a great deal

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:41.874
of vulnerability

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:43.176
about what that was like for her,

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.677
that self-questioning of.

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.546
Did we do enough? Were we enough?

00:05:46.546 --> 00:05:48.181
What does this say about us?

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:49.682
That this is the term the country makes?

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:50.750
And she talks about that

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.617
so incredibly openly.

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:52.752
I have to say,

00:05:52.752 --> 00:05:53.586
she seems like someone

00:05:53.586 --> 00:05:55.621
who really wants to be a helper.

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:56.255
She's providing

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:57.690
tangible things that have worked for her.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:58.591
She talks about picking up

00:05:58.591 --> 00:05:59.692
those knitting needles.

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:00.993
And what that has meant for her

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:01.828
and how that was

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:02.762
something that offered her

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:03.963
calm and steadiness

00:06:03.963 --> 00:06:05.698
in a moment of global instability.

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.600
So I think it was a really interesting

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:08.034
turn for her,

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:08.468
and I'm excited

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:09.802
to see what she has to say about it

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:10.770
as she heads out on her book tour

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:11.804
that starts today.

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.840
Did you think that watching

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:14.774
I don't know if you guys noticed.

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.142
I watch them very closely

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.243
on inauguration day

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:19.612
when Donald Trump became president,

00:06:19.612 --> 00:06:21.481
Donald Trump was inaugurated. And

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.450
I could tell like the dynamic

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:26.285
between her and her husband

00:06:26.285 --> 00:06:27.487
he was like, we can get through this,

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:28.187
we can get through this.

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:30.223
And she was like just like kind of

00:06:30.423 --> 00:06:31.691
she didn't want to really be there.

00:06:31.691 --> 00:06:32.692
She was doing her duty,

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.327
but she was not having a look

00:06:34.327 --> 00:06:35.661
at their face.

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:37.597
Yeah, it's really fascinating.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:37.897
In the book,

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:38.865
she describes her husband

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:39.932
as the greatest disrupting

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:41.167
force of her life

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:41.968
and talks about that

00:06:41.968 --> 00:06:43.369
counterbalance between the two of them.

00:06:43.369 --> 00:06:45.171
And I do remember watching their faces

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:46.606
very closely on Inauguration

00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:47.407
Day from our studios

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.207
just down the street from me

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:49.275
all here in D.C.

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:50.777
and seeing the contrast

00:06:50.777 --> 00:06:51.944
and how they were approaching that,

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.146
even though no words were said

00:06:53.146 --> 00:06:54.247
between the two of them.

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:55.882
It was an incredibly powerful moment.

00:06:55.882 --> 00:06:58.084
I don't think I'll forget Juana Summers.

00:06:58.651 --> 00:06:59.285
I also love

00:06:59.285 --> 00:07:00.520
what she said about the pandemic,

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:01.754
and I think that was really relatable

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:02.722
for people with the feeling

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:03.623
that she felt being it,

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:05.091
being at home, writing this book

00:07:05.091 --> 00:07:06.726
Juana Summers, this is a great interview.

00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:07.860
Thank you so much for sharing

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.562
more of it with us this morning.

00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:10.696
Thank you very much for having me.

